  "query_preview": "..\n(This afternoon through next Wednesday)\nIssued at 1155 AM CST Thu Nov 5 2026\n\nCalm and dry weather currently prevails over the Mid-South with\nweak surface high pressure. Aloft, a jet streak extends from the\nsouthern Plains east into the Appalachian Mountains alongside a\nsubtle shortwave trough over the High Plains in TX/OK/KS. This\nsystem will move east, but will remain at a low amplitude with\nmostly zonal flow through the next couple days. Therefore,\ntemperatures are forecast to hold steady today with highs in the\nupper 60s and low 70s.\n\nAt the surface, a reinforcing cold front will arrive in the\nregion early Friday morning, pushing into another blast of cooler\nair. Fog is less likely Friday morning thanks to the increase in\nwinds, which will also keep lows a bit higher within suboptimal\nradiational cooling conditions. But highs will be pushed cooler\nnonetheless. Most guidance brings highs down into the 60s Friday,\ncontinuing downward into the 50s for most areas by Saturday. Lows\nwill also be impacted with a medium (40% - 60%) chance for\nportions of West TN, eastern AR, and the MO bootheel to see lows\nbelow 35 F with the rest of the region likely to fall to 40 F or\nbelow beginning Sunday morning and lasting into early next week.\n\nThrough the weekend the upper trough and jet streak will exit\neast of the region, tightening up into a more amplified trough\nover the northeast with a new trough becoming cutoff over the\nsouthwestern CONUS and northern Mexico. High pressure behind the\nfront Friday will settle in over the region, leading to a fairly\nstagnant forecast starting on Monday. Temperatures will begin to\ngradually warm without any cold advection, but highs are expected\nto only reach into the 60s with lows rising into the upper 30s\nand low 40s. Through the remainder of the period after Monday,\nensembles are confident that an upper ridge will begin to amplify\nover the Rockies. The upstream impacts from the ridge will result\nin troughing over the eastern CONUS, likely keeping the region at\nnormal or slightly below-normal temperatures late next week with\na dry forecast.",
